<subsection xml:id="subsec-vector-fields">
  <title>Vector Fields</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Until now we have studied scalar fields: functions that assign a number
      to each point in a domain.  A <em>vector field</em> instead assigns a
      <em>vector</em> to each point.  Vector fields arise wherever a physical
      quantity has both magnitude and direction that varies from place to place:
      the velocity of a flowing fluid, the force exerted by gravity, or the
      electric field surrounding a charged particle.  Understanding how to
      describe and manipulate vector fields is the foundation of the rest of
      this section.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Definition: vector field ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-vector-field">
    <title>Vector Field, Continuity, and Differentiability</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>D \subseteq \R^n</m>.  A <term>vector field</term> on <m>D</m>
        is a function
        <me>
          \mathbf{v} \colon D \to \R^n, \qquad
          \mathbf{x} \mapsto \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x})
          = \begin{pmatrix} v_1(\mathbf{x}) \\ \vdots \\ v_n(\mathbf{x}) \end{pmatrix},
        </me>
        that assigns a vector to each point of <m>D</m>.
        The functions <m>v_1, \ldots, v_n\colon D \to \R</m> are called the
        <term>coordinate functions</term> (or components) of <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The vector field <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is called <term>continuous</term>
        (respectively <term>differentiable</term>) if each of its coordinate
        functions <m>v_1, \ldots, v_n</m> is continuous (respectively
        differentiable).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <!-- ─── Remark: physical motivation and visualization ─── -->

  <remark xml:id="rem-vector-field-examples">
    <title>Physical Examples of Vector Fields</title>
    <p>
      Vector fields appear throughout engineering and physics.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Meteorology.</em>
      Wind can be modeled as a velocity vector field
      <m>\mathbf{v}\colon \R^2 \to \R^2</m> over a geographic region:
      at each point <m>(x,y)</m> the vector <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y)</m> gives
      the wind speed and direction.  Weather maps visualize such fields as
      grids of arrows whose length encodes speed and whose direction encodes
      the wind direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Aerodynamics.</em>
      The air flow around a wing is a three-dimensional velocity vector field
      <m>\mathbf{v}\colon \R^3 \to \R^3</m>.  Engineers compute pressure and
      lift from the behavior of this field near the surface of the wing.
    </p>
    <p>
      The figure below shows a simple planar rotation field
      <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y) = \bigl(-y,\, x\bigr)</m> as a representative
      example.  At each grid point an arrow is drawn in the direction of
      <m>\mathbf{v}</m>.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="fig-rotation-field">
      <caption>
        The rotation field <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y) = (-y,\, x)</m>.
        Arrow lengths are proportional to the field magnitude
        <m>\|\mathbf{v}\| = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>, so arrows further from
        the origin are longer.  Every vector is perpendicular to the
        position vector, causing counter-clockwise rotation.  This field
        will later serve as the canonical example of a vector field with
        <em>no</em> antiderivative.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-rotation-field" width="55%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.0]
            \draw[->,gray!60] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right,font=\small] {$x$};
            \draw[->,gray!60] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above,font=\small] {$y$};
            % Draw arrows for v(x,y) = (-y, x); length proportional to field magnitude
            \foreach \xi in {-2,-1.2,-0.4,0.4,1.2,2} {
              \foreach \yi in {-2,-1.2,-0.4,0.4,1.2,2} {
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\vx}{-\yi}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\vy}{\xi}
                \pgfmathsetmacro{\sc}{0.18}
                \draw[->,blue!65!black,thin]
                  (\xi,\yi) -- +(\sc*\vx, \sc*\vy);
              }
            }
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Example: central force field ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-gravitational-force-field">
    <title>Central Force Field (Newton's Law of Gravitation)</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A point mass <m>M</m> located at the origin generates a gravitational
        force field.  According to Newton's law of gravitation, the force on a
        test mass at position <m>\mathbf{x} \in \R^3 \setminus \{\mathbf{0}\}</m>
        is
        <me>
          \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x})
          = -\frac{MG}{\|\mathbf{x}\|^3}\,\mathbf{x},
        </me>
        where <m>G</m> is the universal gravitational constant.
        This can be rewritten as
        <me>
          \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x})
          = -\frac{MG}{\|\mathbf{x}\|^2} \cdot \frac{\mathbf{x}}{\|\mathbf{x}\|},
        </me>
        showing that the force has magnitude <m>MG/\|\mathbf{x}\|^2</m> and
        points in the direction <m>-\mathbf{x}/\|\mathbf{x}\|</m>, i.e.\ toward
        the origin.  This is a <em>central force field</em>: all vectors point
        toward a single center and their length depends only on the distance
        from the center.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Example: review question ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-vector-field-review-question">
    <title>Identifying a Vector Field from Its Properties</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Which of the following vector fields on <m>\R^2</m> has the property
        that <em>at all points with <m>x \neq 0</m>, vectors point away from
        the <m>y</m>-axis</em> and <em>all vectors along any fixed vertical
        line have the same length</em>?
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>(A) <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y) = \begin{pmatrix} x^3 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m></p></li>
        <li><p>(B) <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y) = \begin{pmatrix} x^2 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m></p></li>
        <li><p>(C) <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y) = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ x^3 \end{pmatrix}</m></p></li>
        <li><p>(D) <m>\mathbf{v}(x,y) = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ x^2 \end{pmatrix}</m></p></li>
      </ul>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        <em>Away from the <m>y</m>-axis</em> means the first component
        is positive for <m>x &gt; 0</m> and negative for <m>x &lt; 0</m>.
        This rules out (B), where <m>x^2 \geq 0</m> always, and (C), (D),
        where the horizontal component is zero — the vectors are vertical, not
        pointing away from the axis.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Same length along a vertical line</em> (i.e.\ constant <m>x</m>)
        means the vector length depends only on <m>x</m>, not on <m>y</m>.
        For (A), <m>\|\mathbf{v}(x,y)\| = |x^3|</m>, which depends only on
        <m>x</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The answer is <em>(A)</em>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-vector-fields-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-vector-fields-radial-field">
      <title>Recognise a Radial Field</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which vector field points radially away from the origin in the plane?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>\mathbf{v}(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\end{pmatrix}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. At every point, the vector points in the same direction as the position vector.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\mathbf{v}(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}-y\\x\end{pmatrix}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. That field is tangential to circles around the origin.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\mathbf{v}(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}1\\0\end{pmatrix}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is a constant horizontal field.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\mathbf{v}(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}0\\-1\end{pmatrix}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. This is a constant downward field.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-vector-fields-evaluate-point">
      <title>Evaluate a Vector Field at a Point</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Evaluate
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}(x,y,z)=\begin{pmatrix}y\\-x\\z\end{pmatrix}
          </me>
          at the point <m>(1,2,3)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Substitute <m>x=1</m>, <m>y=2</m>, <m>z=3</m>:
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}(1,2,3)=\begin{pmatrix}2\\-1\\3\end{pmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
